-- title:
--   Existence of the product of two p-divisible groups
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $p$ a prime and $h,h'$ natural numbers, and let $G$ and $H$ be $p$-divisible groups over $R$ of heights $h$ and $h'$ in the sense of the project structure: families $v \mapsto G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of commutative $R$-Hopf algebras that are cocommutative and finite free as $R$-modules, with $R$-rank $p^{v h}$ at level $v$, equipped with surjective bialgebra maps $G.\mathrm{transition}\,v : G.\mathrm{level}(v+1) \to G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ whose kernel is the ideal generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^{v}$ on points (the torsion ideal `torsionIdeal` for $n = p^v$). The assertion is that there exist a $p$-divisible group $P$ over $R$ of height $h+h'$ and two families of $R$-bialgebra maps $iG_v : G.\mathrm{level}\,v \to P.\mathrm{level}\,v$ and $iH_v : H.\mathrm{level}\,v \to P.\mathrm{level}\,v$ such that, for every $v$, $G.\mathrm{transition}\,v$ followed by $iG_v$ equals $iG_{v+1}$ followed by $P.\mathrm{transition}\,v$, and likewise for $H$; and such that for every commutative $R$-algebra $L$ and every $v$, the map sending a point $x \in P.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ (an $R$-algebra map $P.\mathrm{level}\,v \to L$, with the convolution monoid structure) to the pair of its restrictions along $iG_v$ and $iH_v$ is a bijection $P.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v \to G.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v \times H.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$, and each of the two restriction maps carries the convolution product of two points to the product of their restrictions.
--
--   This is the construction of the product $G \times H$ of two $p$-divisible groups, whose level-$v$ coordinate Hopf algebra is the tensor product of those of $G$ and $H$, together with the two projections and the resulting level-by-level identification $P_v(L) \cong G_v(L) \times H_v(L)$ of points, multiplicative for the convolution product. It supplies the ambient group needed when a family of bialgebra maps is to be reconstructed from an additive map on points, and is used in that existence-and-uniqueness result over rings of integers.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PDivisibleGroup_exists_prod_bialgHom_bijective_points.lean

